Q: Finding an operator for 3 distinct values with bitwise operations A: <p>In complement to Sebastian Redl's answer, it is possible to follow a SWAR approach and vectorize up to 32 operators within a <code>std::uint64_t</code> register. The operator becomes:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename T=std::uint64_t&gt;
auto op (T x, T y)  {
    T a_bit  = (x &amp; y) &amp; 0x5555555555555555;
    T a_mask = (a_bit &lt;&lt; 1) | a_bit;
    T b_or_c = (x ^ y) | 0x5555555555555555;
    return b_or_c &amp; a_mask;
}
</code></pre>
<p>One can check the whole thing as follows:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;print&gt;
#include &lt;map&gt;
#include &lt;random&gt;
#include &lt;utility&gt;

using type = std::uint64_t;

constexpr type A=0b00;
constexpr type B=0b01;
constexpr type C=0b11;

template&lt;typename T=type&gt;
auto op (T x, T y)  {
    T a_bit  = (x &amp; y) &amp; 0x5555555555555555;
    T a_mask = (a_bit &lt;&lt; 1) | a_bit;
    T b_or_c = (x ^ y) | 0x5555555555555555;
    return b_or_c &amp; a_mask;
}

int main()
{
    std::map &lt;std::pair&lt;type,type&gt;, type&gt; truth = {
        {{A,A},A}, {{A,B},A}, {{A,C},A},
        {{B,A},A}, {{B,B},B}, {{B,C},C},
        {{C,A},A}, {{C,B},C}, {{C,C},B}
    };

    std::random_device rd;
    std::mt19937 gen(rd());
    std::uniform_int_distribution&lt;&gt; dis(0, 2);

    auto rdm = [&amp;] {
        auto r = dis(gen);
             if (r==0) { return A; }
        else if (r==1) { return B; }
        else if (r==2) { return C; }
        std::unreachable();
    };

    type x={};
    type y={};
    for (std::size_t i=0; i&lt;32; i++) {
        x = (x&lt;&lt;2) | rdm();
        y = (y&lt;&lt;2) | rdm();
    }

    // we compute 32 operators at the same time
    type z = op(x,y);

    // we check that each individual result is ok
    for (std::size_t i=0; i&lt;32; i++) {
        auto [xx,yy,zz] = std::make_tuple (x&amp;0b11, y&amp;0b11, z&amp;0b11);
        std::println (&quot;{:02b} op {:02b} = {:02b}  truth: {:02b}  isok: {}&quot;,
            xx, yy, zz, truth[std::make_pair(xx,yy)], zz==truth[std::make_pair(xx,yy)]
        );
        x &gt;&gt;= 2;
        y &gt;&gt;= 2;
        z &gt;&gt;= 2;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/W3dKG4331" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<p>This scheme should be easily extended to SIMD (see <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/c6Pdah8ca" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>).</p>

Q: From a root node, reaching all the next vertices with a specific label A: <p>You can use <code>all_simple_paths</code> (or <code>all_shortest_paths</code>) + <code>degree</code> like below</p>
<pre><code>f &lt;- function(g, s) {
    unique(
        unlist(
            lapply(
                all_simple_paths(g, s, V(g)[degree(g) == 1]),
                function(x) {
                    if (any(x$color == &quot;red&quot;)) {
                        x[which.max(x$color == &quot;red&quot;)]
                    }
                }
            )
        )
    )
}
</code></pre>
<p>such that</p>
<pre><code>&gt; f(g, 1)
[1] 8 9 5 3

&gt; f(g, 6)
[1]  3 13

&gt; f(g, 11)
[1] 5

&gt; f(g, 7)
[1] 3
</code></pre>

Q: How do you enforce all enum values are keys in an object using zod A: <p>@Souperman's answer is great.</p>
<p>I have personally found this question when I had a similar requirement, but the schema attached to each enum key needed to be more complex than just <code>z.string()</code>. In case someone is looking for a solution to this, which is totally based on the previous answer, you can use this function:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function allEnumKeysSchema&lt;T extends z.ZodEnum&lt;any&gt;, V extends z.ZodTypeAny&gt;(yourEnum: T, innerSchema:V): z.ZodType&lt;{[K in z.infer&lt;T&gt;]: typeof innerSchema }&gt; {
    const schema: z.ZodType&lt;{[K in z.infer&lt;T&gt;]: typeof innerSchema }&gt; = z.object({
        ...Object.keys(yourEnum.enum)
         .reduce((obj, key) =&gt; ({ ...obj, [key]: innerSchema }), {})
    }) as z.ZodTypeAny
    return schema
}

const Schema = allEnumKeysSchema(YOUR_ENUM, YOUR_SCHEMA)
</code></pre>

Q: I&#39;m trying to make a wireframe using CSS Grid and I can&#39;t get the layout to cooperate. Any tips? A: <p>Okay, so...I figured out what was going on.</p>
<p>First, there was a clash of <code>grid template</code> properties. I didn't need to use <code>grid-template-rows</code> and <code>grid-template-columns</code> for my div, so I took those out, and under <code>grid-template-areas</code>, I changed <code>&quot;main-content&quot;</code> to <code>&quot;section&quot;</code>.</p>
<p>Second, I realized that the reason the element sizes inside the <code>div</code> were off was because I was trying to size them relative to the <code>div</code> size and not the grid fractions themselves. Once I realized that, I worked on the sizing, and everything started to fall into place.</p>
<p>Thank you, Paulie, for taking the time to answer my question, and for the snippet tip!</p>
<p>I'm still working on everything, but here is my code so far:</p>
